        <body>
            <div xml:id="d178">

                <pb facs="0317" n="223" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg223"/>

                <head>No. 178.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Denby to Mr. Bayard</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-06-17"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 17, 1887</date>.
                        (Received August 1.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 388.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inclose herewith a
                    translation of the reply of the Tsuug-li yamên to my request that a certificate
                    be granted to Rev. Lai Ki to return to the United States.</p>
                <p>The request was made at the pressing instance of two missionaries of the
                    Presbyterian Board of Foreign Missions residing at San Francisco. The applicant
                    had -resided in San Francisco until February 5, 1880, and had there been
                    employed as a preacher of the Gospel. His services are again as urgently
                    required among his fellow nationals in California in the same capacity.</p>
                <p>It will be seen that without establishing a precedent, but as a “special
                    arrangement,” the yamên courteously grants my request.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles Denby.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d178-a1.xml" xpointer="d178-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
